# 99-dodo-can.rules — stable names for the Dodo's two USB-CAN adapters
# (ODrive Robotics "USB to CAN Adapter", gs_usb driver, USB 1d50:606f).
#
# can0/can1 are enumeration order, not identity: plugging the adapters in another order
# silently swaps the Damiao and ODrive buses. These rules name each adapter by its USB
# serial — the names fleet_config.py uses — and bring its bus up at 1 Mbit/s. Bringing
# a bus up sends nothing, and motor_service never auto-enables a motor.
#
# Install (README "Machine setup"):
#   sudo cp setup/99-dodo-can.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/ && sudo udevadm control --reload
#   then replug both adapters (or reboot)
# Check:  ip -details link show can_dm     # UP, bitrate 1000000; same for can_od
#
# Which serial is which bus is a WIRING fact. Check after any rewiring: the ODrives
# stream cyclic frames on can_od (`candump can_od`); the Damiaos stay silent until
# spoken to, and answer a register read on can_dm (read MST_ID of motor 1, no motion:
# `cansend can_dm 7FF#0100330700000000` -> reply on 0x011). VERIFIED 2026-11-04 on
# TUM770035243-11: ODrive nodes 3/4/7/8 on can_od, Damiao 1/2/5/6 answering on can_dm.
# A replaced adapter: udevadm info -q property /sys/class/net/can0 | grep SERIAL_SHORT
#
# "99-" so it runs after systemd's 80-net-setup-link.rules and its NAME wins.

# Damiao hips (roll + pitch)
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTRS{idProduct}=="606f", ATTRS{serial}=="0027002F4858530731303438", NAME="can_dm", RUN+="/usr/sbin/ip link set can_dm up type can bitrate 1000000"

# ODrive knees + ankles
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", ATTRS{idVendor}=="1d50", ATTRS{idProduct}=="606f", ATTRS{serial}=="003800224858530731303438", NAME="can_od", RUN+="/usr/sbin/ip link set can_od up type can bitrate 1000000"
